sections.body:
Because App Configuration and Key Vault never talk to each other directly, your app must authenticate to both. A typical ASP.NET Core setup connects to App Configuration and separately configures Key Vault access in the same call:
```csharp
builder.Configuration.AddAzureAppConfiguration(options =>
{
    options.Connect(new Uri(endpoint), new DefaultAzureCredential());
    options.ConfigureKeyVault(kv =>
    {
        kv.SetCredential(new DefaultAzureCredential());
    });
});
```
In Azure, `DefaultAzureCredential` resolves to the app's managed identity for both services — you just need to separately grant that identity access to Key Vault (e.g., via RBAC or access policy).

Set these through standard OpenTelemetry resource attributes: ```csharp
var resourceAttributes = new Dictionary<string, object> {
    { "service.name", "my-service" },
    { "service.namespace", "my-namespace" },
    { "service.instance.id", "my-instance" }};
``` Cloud Role Name uses `service.namespace` + `service.name` (falling back to `service.name` alone if namespace isn't set); Cloud Role Instance uses `service.instance.id`. The role name is what shows under each node in the Application Map. If unset, Java defaults Cloud Role Name to the Application Insights resource name and Cloud Role Instance to the machine name.
